# Minutes of Meeting  
**Project:** Data Analyst Mental Health Project Follow-up  
**Date:** August 25, 2026  

## Discussion Summary
- The team discussed building a mental-health risk prediction web application using a trained model saved as a `.pkl` file. 
  Users would enter relevant inputs, and the app would return a prediction and recommendations. (Shivan)
- Potential data sources include Statistics Canada and the WHO mortality database. Some sources were found to provide visual summaries rather than downloadable raw data. (Samir)
- The group agreed to avoid collecting excessive data and focus first on obtaining suitable datasets for KPIs, visualization, and model development.
- Automation was discussed for data extraction and cleaning, including possible web scraping. It will remain optional until the core model is complete.
- For multiple sources, the team plans to compile and clean data into a unified dataset. Cleaning will include deduplication, repeated-record handling, outlier checks, and bias review.
- A shared workflow was proposed: separate folders for Excel-based cleaning and Python work, with GitHub used for version control and data sharing.

## Decisions
- Prioritize the model, KPIs, data cleaning, and visualization before building automation or deploying the web app.
- Each team member will collect assigned datasets, upload them to GitHub, and the team will merge and clean them together.
- Use GitHub Desktop practices: fetch/pull before uploads, add descriptive commit messages, then commit and push.

