# Project Overview and Deployment Plan (Input by Shivan)
- Model saved as a .pkl file, loaded into a web app for mental health risk predictions
- App takes user inputs (days in mental health care, alcoholism, stress, blood pressure, etc.) and returns results with recommendations
- Deployment planned via free hosting (one free page available)
- Timeline: roughly one to two months to completion
- Good portfolio project for the team

# Data Sources and Collection

- Primary source: Statistics Canada (Stats Can)
- WHO mortality database also identified as a data source
  - Contains country, year, ICD codes, sex, age group, and death counts
  - ICD8/ICD9 versioning noted; data goes up to 2002 for some files
- Some sites only have summary visualizations, not raw data
- Risk of getting swamped with too much data flagged

# Automation Strategy

- Automation most useful at the extraction and cleaning stages
- Consensus: keep automation optional for now, focus on building the model first
  - Can add automation as an improvement later

# Data Cleaning and Wrangling Approach

- Multiple data sources mean the team needs to clean and compile into one unified dataset
- Each member collects their own data, then the team merges and cleans together
- Key cleaning steps: deduplication, handling repeated records, outlier and bias detection
- Folder structure proposed: one folder for Excel cleaning, one for Python work
  - Showcases the team’s full-stack data skills

# GitHub Workflow and Data Sharing

- Suggested: if data points overlap across sources after visualization, they are likely duplicates from the same origin
- Team using GitHub Desktop to share data files
  - Always fetch/pull origin before uploading new files
  - Create a personal folder, copy files in, write a commit description, then commit and push
- Survey data uploaded to GitHub during the session as a walkthrough
